In C#, the keyword "static" is used to declare a member (field, property, method, or constructor) or a class as a static member.

When a member is declared as static, it belongs to the class itself, rather than to an instance of the class. This means that there is only one copy of the static member, shared among all instances of the class.

For example, consider the following class:
```csharp
public class MyClass
{
    public static int myStaticField = 0;

    public static void myStaticMethod()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Hello, World!");
    }
}
```
In this example, `myStaticField` is a static field, and `myStaticMethod` is a static method. We can access these members without creating an instance of the class:
```csharp
MyClass.myStaticField = 10; // sets the value of the static field
MyClass.myStaticMethod(); // calls the static method
```
Static members can also be used to implement singleton patterns, where only one instance of a class is allowed to exist at any given time.

In addition, static constructors are used to initialize static fields before any instances of the class are created. A static constructor is called automatically when the class is first accessed, and can only be called once.

